      "payload": {
        "scenario_description": "cabelas",
        "memory_description": "The Cabelas website dynamically changes element IDs for location-based search filters, making it impossible to reliably input or select zip codes for proximity-based product searches.",
        "content_body": "On the Cabelas website, when attempting to search for products within a specific geographic range (e.g., 100 miles of a given zip code), the UI elements used to input or select location data (such as zip codes or radius filters) are rendered with dynamically generated element IDs that change between page loads. This makes it impossible to consistently target the correct input fields using static identifiers. Additionally, the 'within 100 miles' filter may not be explicitly labeled or may be nested within a broader 'location' or 'store finder' section, requiring users to navigate through multiple layers of the UI to apply the filter. To successfully complete the task, the interaction must dynamically detect and apply the 'zip code' and 'radius' filters in real-time, rather than relying on fixed element IDs or assumptions about the UI structure."
      }
    },
